{
  "requirement": "The requirements index MUST show each requirement's evidence state as Valid, Missing, Determination false, FIXME, or Invalid. Valid MUST mean its determination is true and its record passes the same evidence and reference coverage checks as `habeas::validate()`. A false or FIXME determination MUST have its distinct state when the record passes all other checks. The FIXME state MUST pass validation while retaining its distinct label. Malformed or stale evidence MUST remain Invalid regardless of its determination. Each detail page MUST show any validation findings.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the implementation looks right, but the tests miss a branch the requirement states explicitly, so I'm marking it false.**\n\n**Implementation** (all in `src/index.rs`)\n- `LoadedDetailEvidence::load` (154–209) takes the `inspect_repository` report errors that start with `evidence/<name>.json:`. These come from the same checks `validate()` runs: schema, requirement-text staleness, outline and requirements inputs, `no input covers` reference coverage, anchors and example-test coverage.\n- It then derives the state as follows:\n  - Missing when the file is absent.\n  - With no errors: Valid for `true`, Determination false for `false`, FIXME for FIXME, and Invalid when the determination cannot be parsed.\n  - Determination false only when every error is `determination is false…`.\n  - Invalid for any other error set.\n- This matches the text:\n  - A false or FIXME determination keeps its own state only when the record passes everything else.\n  - FIXME produces no validation error, so it passes validation but keeps its label.\n  - Any other finding forces Invalid whatever the determination.\n- `EvidenceState::label` gives the five required labels, and `color` gives the badge colour.\n- `DetailPlan::populate` writes each state into the index table row.\n- `render_detail_sections` shows the badge and a \"Validation findings\" section whenever errors exist.\n- The marking looks complete. The one weak spot is the `EvidenceState` enum (127), which is only `{cite}`d. It holds no behaviour, so I treated it as minor.\n\n**Tests**\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` asserts the index row states Valid, Determination false, Invalid (stale text), Missing and Invalid (uncovered `{impl}` reference). It also checks the Valid badge and that the stale detail page shows the finding.\n- `tests/outcomes.rs` covers FIXME in several ways:\n  - A FIXME record is not flagged as invalid.\n  - FIXME appears in the index and on the detail page (`evidence-FIXME`).\n  - A malformed FIXME and a FIXME with stale inputs both become Invalid, but only in the `status` output.\n  - A FIXME with an uncovered reference is shown to produce a coverage error, though not as an Invalid state.\n\n**Gaps**\n- Nothing tests a record with a `false` determination that also has stale, malformed or uncovered findings and must be Invalid. The requirement says \"Malformed or stale evidence MUST remain Invalid regardless of its determination\".\n  - The `.all(... \"determination is false\")` branch in `load` exists for exactly this case.\n  - I checked the `status` fixture in `tests/commands.rs` and the other `false` fixtures I found. The only `false` fixtures are clean, so they never combine `false` with another finding.\n- The index-level FIXME check is weak. `index.content.contains(\"FIXME\")` does not look at the row state.\n\nThere are no `FIXME(...)` markers acknowledging these gaps, so the answer is false rather than FIXME.",
